{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/11/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c3fa4749\" lines=\"79-96\">\n<description>\nRecolor each side-marked multicolor object embedded in a monochrome panel to its marker color.\n\nInput structure:\n- Same-color 4-connected components form monochrome panel regions.\n- The bounding box of a panel may contain 4-connected non-panel-color objects.\n- A target object touches exactly one side of its panel bbox, and all of its\n  pixels on that side have one marker color.\n\nRule:\n- For every target object, fill any panel-colored holes enclosed by the object.\n- Recolor the object and filled holes to the side marker color.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- A multicolor object's single contact side identifies both which object should\n  be normalized and which color should replace it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1d61978c\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nRecolor the two diagonal foreground-bar orientations, assigning teal to the larger orientation and red to the smaller one.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground pixels form one-cell-thick diagonal bars.\n- Every foreground pixel belongs to exactly one diagonal orientation.\n\nRule\n- Keep the background unchanged.\n- Classify each foreground pixel by whether it has a diagonal neighbor on the NW/SE axis or the NE/SW axis.\n- Recolor the orientation with more foreground pixels teal and the other orientation red.\n\nKey insight\n- The output colors depend on the relative population of the two diagonal orientations, not on a fixed slash direction or position.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad3b40cf\" lines=\"72-87\">\n<description>\nMirror the smallest non-axis foreground color across the full straight axis.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color forms exactly one full row, column, main diagonal, or anti-diagonal reflection axis.\n- The remaining foreground colors include a unique least-populated color to copy.\n\nRule\n- Preserve the input grid.\n- Select every pixel of the unique smallest non-axis foreground color.\n- Reflect those pixels across the full straight axis and paint the reflected positions with the same color.\n\nKey insight\n- The axis color marks the mirror line, while the color population chooses which set of objects is duplicated.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2601afb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nRotate each vertical bar's color one slot right and its height one slot left.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background surrounds several monochrome one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Every bar is bottom-aligned and occupies a distinct column.\n- The ordered bar slots are read from left to right.\n\nRule\n- Keep the grid shape and the bar columns unchanged.\n- At slot i, use the color from the previous slot cyclically.\n- At slot i, use the height from the next slot cyclically.\n\nKey insight\n- Color and height are independent cyclic attributes of the ordered bar slots:\n  colors rotate right while heights rotate left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"396d80d7\" lines=\"18-30\">\n<description>\nPaint the inner foreground color into every background pixel that touches the enclosing frame only diagonally.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color is an enclosing frame: its bbox strictly contains the bbox of the other, inner color.\n\nRule:\n- Keep all existing pixels unchanged.\n- For each background pixel, if it is diagonally adjacent to the frame color and has no orthogonal adjacency to the frame color, recolor it to the inner color.\n\nKey insight: The inner color marks the color to use, while the frame's diagonal-only background neighbors define the cells to add.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3bd292e8\" lines=\"13-29\">\n<description>\nRecolor wall-separated background regions alternately, starting with grey in the lower-left region.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground wall color.\n- The wall pixels form one or more 4-connected one-cell-thick segments.\n- Each wall segment separates exactly two 4-connected background regions.\nRule\n- Keep all wall pixels unchanged.\n- Label the 4-connected background regions and connect two labels when they\n  touch opposite sides of the same wall segment.\n- Color the region containing the lower-left corner grey, then flip between\n  grey and green across every wall adjacency.\nKey insight\n- The red walls define a bipartite map of regions; the lower-left background\n  region fixes which side of the alternating coloring is grey.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22806e14\" lines=\"23-37\">\n<description>\nMark the center of each odd-sized solid square with the plus marker color, erasing the marker only when it is outside the marked-center field.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is background.\n- Exactly two foreground colors are present: one unique 3x3 plus marker and one color forming separated solid square objects.\n\nRule:\n- For every non-marker 4-connected object, assert it is a solid square.\n- If the square side length is odd, recolor its center pixel to the plus marker color; even-sided squares stay unchanged.\n- Preserve the plus marker when its center lies inside the bounding box of the odd-square centers; otherwise erase it to background.\n\nKey insight:\n- Odd-sided solid squares have a single visible center pixel, and the plus supplies the color used to identify those centers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"dce56571\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nStraighten the single foreground mask into a centered horizontal segment of the same color and length.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- All non-background pixels share one foreground color.\n- The number of foreground pixels fits in one centered row of the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Erase the original foreground mask.\n- Count its pixels, keep its color, and paint that many pixels as a horizontal segment.\n- Place the segment on the middle row with equal background margin on the left and right.\n\nKey insight:\n- The original foreground shape is irrelevant except for its color and area; the output is the area normalized into the grid's central horizontal line.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9841fdad\" lines=\"15-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the colored runs from the left reference panel into the blank right panel.\n\nInput structure:\n- A uniform rectangular frame surrounds the grid.\n- One full-height divider of the frame color splits a left reference panel\n  from a blank right panel.\n- Both panels have one background margin column on each side of their active\n  columns; the left active columns contain two-column endpoint slots, possibly\n  connected by same-color horizontal runs.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the original grid and paint only in the right panel's active columns.\n- A source run exactly matching the left endpoint slot is copied to the left\n  two columns of the right active span.\n- A source run exactly matching the right endpoint slot is copied to the right\n  two columns of the right active span.\n- A source run connecting the endpoint slots is stretched across the entire\n  right active span.\n\nKey insight:\nThe left panel is a compressed legend for the right panel: endpoint markers\nstay two pixels wide, while connectors between endpoints expand to fill the\ndistance between them.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\" lines=\"209-224\">\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.\n- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.\n- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.\n- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.\n- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.\n\nKey insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d93c6891\" lines=\"84-101\">\n<description>\nMove each gray bar into its adjacent orange rectangle as full stripes from the contacted edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The scene uses black outside space, yellow fill, gray one-cell-thick bars, and solid orange rectangles.\n- Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.\n- Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.\n\nRule\n- Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.\n- For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
